WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.600
Jeremy Ramey: I thought that the court system,
legal system, was going to be fighting for

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.890
the kids.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.930
And I quickly found out that's not the case.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:24.590
What do I love most about being a daddy?

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.220
Do we have all day?

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.810
Because I can go on and on and on.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:32.200
I knew i always wanted to be a dad first of
all.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.589
That was like a non-negotiable for me.

00:00:34.589 --> 00:00:37.829
And we grow up, I got married.

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:42.360
And when two guys get married it's kind of
hard to have a kid!

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:47.800
We had friends who fostered and they had like
the perfect foster story.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.390
And so we got our foster license.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:59.569
Arlem Garcia: You would think that kids that
don't know who you are, coming into your house,

00:00:59.569 --> 00:01:03.120
they're coming and meeting you, they're going
to be shy and they're going to be, you know,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.120
withdrawn.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.920
And they just have gone through whatever traumatic
thing coming into foster care.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.140
And it wasn't like that.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:15.500
They just hugged us and immediately just,
just held on like they had known us forever.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.369
And it was amazing.

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:18.369
That was it!

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:19.479
It was it was over for us then.

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:20.799
Like, this is our life.

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:24.350
Naomi Schaefer Riley: Jeremy and Arlem took
in these kids.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.170
They made them part of their family.

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.570
But these kids came to them with very difficult
histories.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:36.320
They had bruises and burns on them.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:42.000
What should matter in terms of how kids are
placed is that they are placed in a stable,

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.799
loving home where they can move toward permanency.

00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:48.520
And we have laws in this country about that.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:54.359
In the 90s, a bipartisan group of lawmakers
came together and passed the Adoption and

00:01:54.359 --> 00:02:00.369
Safe Families Act, and it said if you have
been in foster care for 15 of the last 22

00:02:00.369 --> 00:02:04.729
months, the state should be moving to terminate
parental rights.

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:07.640
And we regularly flout this law.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:14.740
For years, children in foster care spend an
average of more than two years in foster care,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:18.370
and in many cases they are spending three,
four, five years.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.370
Antonio Garcia-Ramey: Yeah?

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:20.370
Arlem Garcia: Mmm hmm.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.370
So put it together.

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.370
Antonio Garcia-Ramey: I can't....

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:23.370
Arlem Garcia: It's re...

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.370
Antonio Garcia-Ramey: Ready?

00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:25.370
Arlem Garcia: See?

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.990
You say you can't but you just did Antonio
Garcia-Ramey: And gets ready for...

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:32.410
Arlem Garcia: Mmm hmm

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.550
Naomi Schaefer Riley: The mother wanted this
child back.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:41.180
Or she would say she did, but she was not
willing to live with any of the conditions

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.320
that the court set.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:47.050
Jeremy Ramey: I thought that everybody, the
court system, legal system, was going to be

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.400
fighting for the kids.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.700
And I quickly found out after the first time
we were in court that's not the case.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:59.300
Arlem Garcia: Every appointment that they
had, every visit that they had, it was completely

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:06.590
based around the parent and whether they could
make it, whether they had a ride whether they

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.450
were feeling up to it that day.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:12.840
They gave her chances to go and get drug tested,
she wouldn't show.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.960
When it came to actual visits, she would go
in blocks where she would see them, you know,

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:19.390
maybe two weeks in a row and then we wouldn't
see her for two months.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.250
And it just went on this way every time we
went to court.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.600
It was just excuse after excuse, chance after
chance.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.720
And there was never a point where someone
said, "Enough is enough!

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.090
Like, you're not doing anything to actually
get your kids back."

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.420
Naomi Schaefer Riley: The idea that judges
regularly dismiss or don't even listen to

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:42.070
people who have cared for vulnerable children
for a year, two years, three years is not

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.900
a recipe for success!

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.390
We need to make foster parents aware that
they actually have a legal right to speak

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:53.790
up in most court hearings, and that the judge
should be listening to what they have to say.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:57.750
Arlem Garcia: So the next court date they
said, you know, mom showed up for visits the

00:03:57.750 --> 00:03:59.790
last three weeks.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:04.620
All of a sudden, mom's attorney files to change
the case plan.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:07.470
The case plan has been severance for two and
a half years.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:13.800
They file based on, like, three weeks of her
interaction to change it to reunification.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:14.800
And the judge agrees.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:19.830
I mean, I'm sitting in the courtroom and my
heart sinks.

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:24.690
We had built this life and this family, and
the system just decided to just pluck him

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:29.060
out of it and put him in a situation that
he should never have been in.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:41.190
And we should have never been in that position
where they could do that.

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:45.930
Eventually during some visits there were some
questions about mom's parenting.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:50.590
And then there was one instance where finally
someone outside the home called in a complaint.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.390
And that person found something.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:53.390
We don't know what it is!

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:54.390
We still don't know what it is.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:55.840
They never said anything.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:56.840
They found something.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:57.840
They removed Antonio.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.690
Jeremy Ramey: So Antonio came home.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:02.139
There's so many tears.

00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:07.240
But the thing that really hurt the most was
Antonio really didn't know who we were for

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.240
a minute.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.650
And he was a little different.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:11.610
He was a little different.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.460
Then he started talking about having to hide
from the bad guys.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:20.190
And it was just heartbreaking that he had
to hide from the bad guys and listen to the

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.190
gunshots.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.060
Arlem Garcia: He was just afraid of everything.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.180
He was afraid to be left alone.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.770
Like, if he didn't see Jeremy or I, like,
visually in a room, like, he would go looking

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:29.770
for us.

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:33.420
I mean, we couldn't be in the bathroom for
five minutes without him like screaming out

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:34.450
yelling out for us.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:39.620
He just wasn't the same kid.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:56.370
Naomi Schaefer Riley: Their story did end
happily and they were able to bring him home.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:02.740
But I think it's also important to think about
the trauma that this child experienced.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:09.889
To have the first years of your life be in
this constant back and forth and never knowing

00:06:09.889 --> 00:06:14.520
where your real home is and where your real
family is and that you're going to be kept

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:15.520
safe.

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:16.520
Arlem Garcia: I still love you
Antonio Garcia-Ramey: We're on the same team!

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:20.920
Arlem Garcia: I still love you
Antonio Garcia-Ramey: We're on the same team!

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:21.021
Arlem Garcia: I still love you
Antonio Garcia-Ramey: We're on the same team!

